              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The question of whether Crimea belongs to Russia is answered differently depending on whether you look at the situation through **international law** (*de jure*) or **actual physical control** (*de facto*).\n\n### 1. International Law (*De Jure*): Crimea belongs to Ukraine\nAccording to international law and the vast majority of the global community, **Crimea is an integral part of Ukraine**. \n* **The United Nations:** Following Russia's military intervention and the subsequent referendum in March 2014, the United Nations General Assembly passed Resolution 68/262. This resolution declared the referendum invalid, affirmed the territorial integrity of Ukraine, and called on all states not to recognize any alteration of Crimea's status.\n* **Global Recognition:** The United States, the European Union, NATO, and over 100 countries explicitly recognize Crimea as Ukrainian territory under Russian occupation. Only a handful of countries (such as North Korea, Syria, and Venezuela) have recognized Russia's claim over the peninsula.\n\n### 2. Practical Control (*De Facto*): Crimea is administered by Russia\nOn the ground, **Russia exercises total physical and administrative control over Crimea**.\n* **Annexation:** In March 2014, Russian forces seized control of the peninsula. Following a highly contested referendum\u2014which Ukraine and international observers dismissed as illegal and held under military occupation\u2014Russia formally annexed Crimea, declaring it a federal subject of the Russian Federation.\n* **Governance:** Russia has integrated Crimea into its own political, legal, economic, and monetary systems. It operates Russian schools, courts, police forces, and military bases on the peninsula. \n\n### Summary\n* **Legally (*de jure*):** Crimea belongs to **Ukraine**, as recognized by the UN and international law.\n* **Practically (*de facto*):** Crimea is occupied, controlled, and administered by **Russia**.",
